WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:02.934
-Our first guest tonight is an Emmy-nominated actress

00:00:02.934 --> 00:00:04.667
you know from her work in the critically-acclaimed shows

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:06.667
"Orange is the New Black" and "Russian Doll."

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:08.334
She executive produces and stars

00:00:08.334 --> 00:00:10.200
in the Peacock series "Poker Face."

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.267
New episodes stream Thursdays. Let's take a look.

00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:15.601
-We were political prisoners.

00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:16.667
-Ah, no [bleep].

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:18.400
-No [bleep] whatsoever.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.901
-How'd you get into that?

00:00:19.901 --> 00:00:23.934
-I was in college. It was a turbulent time.

00:00:23.934 --> 00:00:25.934
-Mm. Oh, yeah. I mean, the '60s.

00:00:25.934 --> 00:00:27.100
-Did you hear that?

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.501
-'70s, bitch. -Oh, right.

00:00:28.501 --> 00:00:31.400
Yeah, I was gonna say the '90s 'cause of all the flannel.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.067
-You'd have been better off going there.

00:00:33.067 --> 00:00:36.267
-Please welcome back to the show our friend Natasha Lyonne!

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:38.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:46.834
♪♪

00:00:46.834 --> 00:00:55.534
♪♪

00:00:55.534 --> 00:00:58.033
Welcome back, Natasha! I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:58.033 --> 00:01:00.334
-Thank you. I know. I am happy to be here.

00:01:00.334 --> 00:01:01.868
-It's very lovely to have you here

00:01:01.868 --> 00:01:03.701
for a show that is just wonderful, as well.

00:01:03.701 --> 00:01:06.167
We had the show's -- one of the show's creators.

00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:08.100
Obviously, Rian Johnson, who wrote and directed it.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.701
And he's wonderful. And we were talking about --

00:01:10.701 --> 00:01:12.767
This is sort of a throwback to those sort of

00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:14.701
"Columbo" style shows. -Yeah.

00:01:14.701 --> 00:01:17.300
-You play someone who can tell when people are lying,

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:18.801
which is very good casting.

00:01:18.801 --> 00:01:20.367
'Cause of all my friends, I feel like

00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:21.834
you have the best bull [bleep] detector.

00:01:21.834 --> 00:01:23.234
-Oh, thank you. -Yeah.

00:01:23.234 --> 00:01:25.334
-That was a lie, Seth.

00:01:25.334 --> 00:01:27.133
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:29.467
[ Cheers and applause ] It's a weird...

00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:31.434
-But I -- You've got to think

00:01:31.434 --> 00:01:32.634
you have a good bull [bleep] detector.

00:01:32.634 --> 00:01:35.267
-Well, as a child, uh...

00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:38.467
You know, I ran a small gang.

00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:42.367
Uh, the Bugsy Malone gang. [ Laughter ]

00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:45.000
-You -- How old were you when you ran your first gang?

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.501
-You guys know them, I guess.

00:01:46.501 --> 00:01:47.667
We were notorious.

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:51.667
Uh, I was about 7 to 10.

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:54.200
It's all a blur back then.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:00.234
And, yeah, so, as a young pickpocket, I --

00:02:00.234 --> 00:02:03.467
It was I guess just a skill you learned out in the streets.

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:04.601
-Yeah.

00:02:04.601 --> 00:02:06.601
-You had to be street-wise, you see?

00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:08.901
That's what it was like back then.

00:02:08.901 --> 00:02:11.868
And -- But, you know, the city, it's always changing.

00:02:11.868 --> 00:02:14.167
Gentrification.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:17.067
-Good shift into gentrification.

00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:19.868
-Yeah, I wanted to seem like I was from modern times.

00:02:19.868 --> 00:02:21.100
Yes.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.133
-You're talking about your street-urchin gang

00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:24.968
that was picking pockets.

00:02:24.968 --> 00:02:26.801
-Yeah, I didn't want to seem too old-timey.

00:02:26.801 --> 00:02:29.033
-Yeah. Oh, nobody ever thinks you seem too old-timey.

00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:31.000
-I just don't want it to be alienating.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.501
I am going to say things now like "gaslighting"

00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:35.334
just to bring it in. -Oh, good.

00:02:35.334 --> 00:02:37.434
Because gaslighting -- I know people say it in modern times,

00:02:37.434 --> 00:02:39.000
but I don't think of that as a modern term,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.767
since it came from a '50s movie.

00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:42.601
-No, it's a famous -- Yeah, a big famous -- Yes.

00:02:42.601 --> 00:02:44.567
That's the reason I know the word.

00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:46.434
Yes.

00:02:46.434 --> 00:02:47.734
-That is the embarrassing thing.

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:49.801
When people start using something in social media,

00:02:49.801 --> 00:02:51.334
and you're like, "From the 1950s film?"

00:02:51.334 --> 00:02:53.534
And they're like, "You're an old man."

00:02:53.534 --> 00:02:56.467
-Yeah, they don't like that. Keep it young, Seth, okay?

00:02:56.467 --> 00:02:59.167
I asked you for one thing here tonight.

00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:00.434
-Keep it young. -Yeah.

00:03:00.434 --> 00:03:02.834
I do everything I do for the tweens.

00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:04.601
Yeah.

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:06.133
-"Eyes on Tweens." That's your --

00:03:06.133 --> 00:03:07.234
-"Eyes on Tweens."

00:03:07.234 --> 00:03:08.400
-You have a new show that's on PBS.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.000
-On PBS. It's a -- Ken Burns created it.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.167
-Oh! That's great.

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:14.367
-It's his first foray into fiction.

00:03:14.367 --> 00:03:15.734
-Oh, wow! -It's exciting.

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:18.968
-Now, you, um -- You don't care for --

00:03:18.968 --> 00:03:21.033
'Cause I don't consider what we're doing right now

00:03:21.033 --> 00:03:22.400
sort of classic small talk.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.634
You are not a small-talk aficionado.

00:03:24.634 --> 00:03:26.200
-Don't believe in it. -You don't believe in it.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.667
-Nope.

00:03:27.667 --> 00:03:30.567
I, uh -- I would say I aggressively don't like it.

00:03:30.567 --> 00:03:35.234
I think -- I think it's damaging to society as a whole.

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:38.300
-Do you feel this way about receiving it as far --

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.200
as also for asking it?

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.567
-Yeah, I don't like it in any of the directions.

00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:44.033
-Okay.

00:03:44.033 --> 00:03:48.434
-And I -- It's like John Lennon says. "Just give me some truth."

00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:49.934
And I've come here tonight

00:03:49.934 --> 00:03:52.000
to let you know that those are my interests.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.367
To become -- I want to age into a sort of

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:56.801
Bob Dylan type figure and say things like that.

00:03:56.801 --> 00:03:58.968
But I think it's really dangerous.

00:03:58.968 --> 00:04:02.033
Because if you say to somebody, "How are you?"

00:04:02.033 --> 00:04:04.667
their only option is to lie aggressively, right?

00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:07.033
Like, society says you're supposed to say,

00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:09.067
"Oh, I'm good," and keep it moving.

00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:12.634
But you're not good, are you? I mean, sometimes, rarely.

00:04:12.634 --> 00:04:17.067
But you should say something more like, you know, "50/50."

00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:18.567
And --

00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:21.667
Then the other person could respond with, like,

00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:24.234
"Me? It's a great day. 70/30."

00:04:24.234 --> 00:04:29.234
And on a human-condition level, I think we'd all be well-served.

00:04:29.234 --> 00:04:32.100
It would be more honest. We'd keep it moving.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.934
We could eliminate "How was your weekend?" entirely.

00:04:34.934 --> 00:04:36.234
-Yeah.

00:04:36.234 --> 00:04:38.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:38.601 --> 00:04:40.767
-So you agree? [ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:40.767 --> 00:04:43.000
-Now, do you think -- Can I make an argument for --

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.601
"How is your weekend?"

00:04:44.601 --> 00:04:47.000
An argument for it being a thing you say

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.267
when I walk into an office with one of my coworkers --

00:04:49.267 --> 00:04:51.067
A thing to say just so

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:53.367
I don't immediately start talking [bleep] and gossiping.

00:04:53.367 --> 00:04:54.901
-Right. -You know what I mean?

00:04:54.901 --> 00:04:56.334
It's just like an opener

00:04:56.334 --> 00:04:58.567
just before we actually get into the nitty-gritty.

00:04:58.567 --> 00:05:01.300
-Sure. Uh, listen.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.434
Society functions in some ways, Seth.

00:05:03.434 --> 00:05:05.767
-Yeah. -For example, traffic lights.

00:05:05.767 --> 00:05:07.434
They're helpful.

00:05:07.434 --> 00:05:10.267
That's what I'm sort of hearing from you in this moment,

00:05:10.267 --> 00:05:11.501
is a traffic light.

00:05:11.501 --> 00:05:13.067
-Yeah, I want a little bit of signaling.

00:05:13.067 --> 00:05:16.801
-Yes, but might I propose...

00:05:16.801 --> 00:05:19.868
the idea of a third thing. -Uh-huh.

00:05:19.868 --> 00:05:21.334
-In other words, arguably,

00:05:21.334 --> 00:05:23.400
it doesn't have to be gossip at all.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.934
Maybe it would just be a third thing,

00:05:25.934 --> 00:05:27.868
sort of a topic, you know?

00:05:27.868 --> 00:05:29.934
Like, "Oh, great microphone."

00:05:29.934 --> 00:05:32.367
You'd talk about the microphone for a while.

00:05:32.367 --> 00:05:36.200
And then you'd move on to the pencil and walk away.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:37.901
-So, anything not to ask

00:05:37.901 --> 00:05:40.334
about how you're doing or your weekend.

00:05:40.334 --> 00:05:43.801
Just drill down on the inanimate objects you can see.

00:05:43.801 --> 00:05:46.567
-That's what I like. I actually -- You know, today,

00:05:46.567 --> 00:05:48.200
I've been struggling with inanimate objects.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.033
We talked about it backstage.

00:05:50.033 --> 00:05:51.667
So maybe that's a -- -It's so nice you're being

00:05:51.667 --> 00:05:53.133
honest with the audience about the fact.

00:05:53.133 --> 00:05:54.868
-I know. -Natasha told me backstage -- -

00:05:54.868 --> 00:05:56.767
-I had to listen to Marvin Gaye to get back in the zone

00:05:56.767 --> 00:05:58.534
before I came out here. -You were worried they were

00:05:58.534 --> 00:06:00.400
going to be props out here, because you were certain

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.100
you'd drop them. -Yes.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:05.634
And -- But as soon as I told you, I felt a sense of relief

00:06:05.634 --> 00:06:08.000
of now that probably won't happen.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.567
And those are the kinds of conversations

00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:11.968
people should be having. -Yes, there was value to that.

00:06:11.968 --> 00:06:15.334
I have a lot more to ask you about your wonderful show.

00:06:15.334 --> 00:06:19.133
We will be right back with the one and the only Natasha Lyonne.

